\section{Entity types}{

\itemize{
\item \code{"state"} (195 codes): a current member of the COW state system. The
195 codes cover 194 of COW's 195 current members; Kosovo has no GDELT
code.
\item \code{"former_state"} (3): a code that is a state only in earlier years:
\code{GME} (East Germany, 1954-1990), \code{GMW} (West Germany, 1955-1990) and
\code{SCG} (Serbia and Montenegro, 1992-2006).
\item \code{"territory"} (42): an ISO 3166-1 code with no COW state, e.g. \code{HKG},
\code{MAC}, \code{PRI}, \code{SJM}, \code{GRL}, and the Palestinian codes \code{PSE}, \code{WSB}
(West Bank) and \code{GZS} (Gaza Strip), and Western Sahara (\code{ESH}).
\item \code{"region"} (25): a continent, sub-region or other grouping, e.g. \code{EUR}
(Europe), \code{AFR}, \code{WAF} (West Africa), \code{MEA} (Middle East), \code{WST} (the
West).
\item \code{"other"} (2): \code{VAT} (Vatican City, sovereign but not a COW member)
and \code{BAG} (Baghdad, a city code in the lookup).
}
}

\section{States}{

A code is a state in year \eqn{t} when it maps to a COW state system
member at any point during \eqn{t}, so the rule is time-varying:
Slovakia (\code{SVK}) is a state from 1993, Timor-Leste (\code{TMP}) from 2002,
South Sudan (\code{SSD}) from 2011. Two spells of one COW state number are
merged when they are contiguous at year level. In a year with two
candidate periods for one code, the period holding on 31 December wins
(Germany in 1990 is COW 255, not 260). The COW data (v2024) end on
2024-12-31; a state that is a member on that date is a current member
(\code{cow_end = NA}), taken as a member through 2025 and after.
}

\section{Harmonization}{

State codes are harmonized to ISO 3166-1 alpha-3, so that dyad keys
match the \link{theta_trajectories} tables: \code{ROM} (CAMEO's code for Romania)
becomes \code{ROU}, \code{TMP} becomes \code{TLS} and \code{MTN} becomes \code{MNE}; every other
current-state code already is its ISO3 code. One code is used per COW
state number and period. Where COW keeps one number through a change
of name, the current code is used throughout: \code{SRB} for COW 345
(Yugoslavia, Serbia and Montenegro, Serbia) in every year, and \code{RUS}
for COW 365 (the Soviet Union and Russia).
}

\section{Successor rules}{

\itemize{
\item \strong{Serbia} (\code{SRB}, \code{SCG}): COW 345 is one state from 1944 on; both
codes harmonize to \code{SRB}, so Serbia's series is continuous. \code{SCG} is a
state only in 1992-2006.
\item \strong{Czechoslovakia} (\code{CZE}): GDELT has no Czechoslovakia code and
writes \code{CZE} for Czechoslovak actors too, so \code{CZE} before 1993 is
Czechoslovakia (COW 315, ISO3 \code{CSK}) and from 1993 the Czech Republic
(316). Slovakia (\code{SVK}, 317) is a state from 1993.
\item \strong{Germany} (\code{DEU}, \code{GMW}, \code{GME}): before 1990 GDELT's \code{DEU} is West
Germany (COW 260); from 1990, Germany (255). Both harmonize to \code{DEU}.
\code{GMW} (West Germany, 260) is \code{DEU} and \code{GME} (East Germany, 265) is
\code{DDR}, through 1990.
\item \strong{Yemen} (\code{YEM}): before 1990, North Yemen (COW 678); from 1990,
Yemen (679). South Yemen (680) has no GDELT code.
\item \strong{Russia} (\code{RUS}): COW 365 throughout; GDELT has no Soviet Union
code.
\item \strong{Others:} Croatia (\code{HRV}) and Bosnia and Herzegovina (\code{BIH}) are
states from 1992, North Macedonia (\code{MKD}) and Eritrea (\code{ERI}) from
1993, Palau (\code{PLW}) from 1994, Montenegro (\code{MTN}) from 2006; Sudan
(\code{SDN}) is continuous through South Sudan's secession; \code{COD} covers
Zaire and \code{MMR} covers Burma.
}
}

\section{Contested cases}{

Contested and borderline cases follow the COW rule. \strong{Taiwan} (\code{TWN},
COW 713) is a state. \strong{Kosovo} (COW 347, a member from 2008-02-20) is a
state, but GDELT 1.0 has no code for it. \strong{Palestine} (\code{PSE}, \code{WSB},
\code{GZS}) and \strong{Western Sahara} (\code{ESH}) are territories. \strong{Vatican City}
(\code{VAT}) is not a COW member; the COW number 327 that some crosswalks
give it is the Papal States (1816-1860).
}

\section{Codes GDELT does not write}{

GDELT 1.0 derives the country code from the leading country segment of
the actor code (\code{Actor1Code}), and leaves the country code empty when
its own country table lacks that segment. Seven such prefixes are
listed here, with \code{name_source = "added"} where GDELT has no label:
\code{ROU} (Romania: actors are coded \code{ROU...}, but GDELT's table has
\code{ROM}), \code{TWN} (Taiwan, in the backfiles to March 2013), \code{SSD} (South
Sudan, in the backfiles), \code{SCG}, \code{GME}, \code{GMW} and \code{GGY} (Guernsey).
\code{\link[=read_gdelt]{read_gdelt()}}'s \code{recover_country_codes = TRUE} fills an empty country
code from such a prefix.

Four states have no code GDELT 1.0 ever writes, as a country code or
as an actor-code prefix, in any year: \strong{Bosnia and Herzegovina},
\strong{Slovenia} and \strong{Montenegro} (\code{BIH}, \code{SVN} and \code{MTN} are in the
lookup but never occur) and \strong{Kosovo} (no code at all). Their actors
are coded by role only (e.g. \code{GOV}, \code{MIL}), so their events cannot be
assigned to them from the country codes, and state-to-state event
tables built from GDELT 1.0 do not include them.
}

